 "amendments": {
  "items": {
   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the education law, in relation to the requirements for a\nlicense as a professional engineer; and to repeal certain provisions of such\nlaw relating thereto",
    "basePrintNo": "S3541",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S3541-2021",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §7206, rpld §7206 sub 2, Ed L",
    "lawSection": "Education Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S3541\n\nSPONSOR: STAVISKY\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the education law, in relation to the requirements for a\nlicense as a professional engineer; and to repeal certain provisions of\nsuch law relating thereto\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nThis legislation updates the engineering licensing requirements by\nremoving the provision allowing for a substitution of 12 years working\nexperience for a bachelor's degree or higher based on a program in engi-\nneering. Persons with substantially equivalent educational credentials,\nin accordance with the commissioner's regulations, will also qualify to\nsit for the PE examination, provided all other requirements for licen-\nsure are satisfied. The legislation also changes reference to \"intern\nengineers\" to \"engineers in training\" and permits students in engineer-\ning and engineering technology programs approved by the commissioner of\neducation to sit for the fundamentals of engineering examination in\naccordance with the commissioner's regulations. Currently a student must\nwait until he or she is within 20 hours of graduation. This bill elimi-\nnates the current timeframe and vests the commissioner with discretion\nto fix the timeframe. Additionally, the Department of Education is\nempowered to waive the educational requirement for applicants who have\n15 years of lawful practice and possess established and recognized\nstanding in the profession. The legislation also contains some technical\namendments respecting examination fees. Finally, a person seeking licen-\nsure pursuant to the current 12 year experience ladder is authorized to\nfile an application within 2 years of the effective date of the legis-\nlation in order to preserve the ability to qualify for engineering\nlicensure based on experience.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nToday, very few individuals secure a professional engineering license\nwithout a formal education including a bachelor's degree in engineering.\nHowever, a bachelor's degree is not required under current law. The bill\nis consistent with the standard used throughout the nation and reflects\nthe complexity of the demands placed on the engineering community. The\npractice of engineering has developed over the years into a highly tech-\nnical profession requiring extensive knowledge of math, science, and\nengineering. Due to the highly technical and complex nature of engineer-\ning, it is now necessary to have an education requirement as part of the\nrequirements for licensure as a professional engineer. This revised\neducation requirement for engineers will help ensure the health safety\nand welfare of the public, as engineers are responsible for the infras-\ntructure around us. An engineer can no longer obtain the necessary foun-\ndation in math, science, and engineering from a high school education\nand twelve years of experience.\n\nToday's design for buildings, roads, bridges, water and wastewater\nfacilities, etc. require a foundation in math, science, and engineering\nthat can only be provided through a bachelors degree or higher in engi-\nneering or engineering technology. Individuals can secure a bachelor's\ndegree in engineering in New York via a variety of programs. A number of\ncommunity colleges and 4 year SUNY institutions offer bridge programs\nthat are affordable and provide the opportunity to transfer into the\nnumerous NYS 4 four year licensure qualifying engineering or engineering\ntechnology programs.  In order not to disenfranchise those individuals\nin the pipeline, a limited exception is provided for individuals who\nhave embarked on a career path in reliance upon the 12 year ladder.\nIndividuals seeking a license based upon the 12 year ladder provision\nwill be afforded 2 years following the date of enactment to file an\napplication with the Education Department thereby preserving their right\nto utilize this alternative approach to a license at any time in the\nfuture.  Additionally, the State Education Department is authorized to\npermit licensure for engineers who have established and recognized\nstanding in the engineering profession but do not technically satisfy\nNew York's educational requirement, provided they have not less than 15\nyears of lawful practice, typically in a sister state.\n\nIn another update to existing law, the current timeframe for a student\nto qualify to sit for the Fundamentals of Engineering Examination is\neliminated and authority is vested in the commissioner to fix the time\nin order to assist students as they approach completion of their under-\ngraduate degree programs. National norms now allow students to sit for\nthe examination much sooner in their college careers.  Additionally the\nchange in the title \"intern engineer\" to \"engineer in training\" more\naptly describes those on the path to licensure as a professional engi-\nneer. A technical amendment in the bill deletes reference to examination\nfees paid to third parties for testing services.\n\nThe practice of professional engineering is increasingly complex, and\nthe vast majority of engineers have, at minimum, a 4 year degree in\nengineering. The legislation will update the licensing requirements to\nreflect modern practice requirements and promote uniformity for the\nprofession.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nA9694 of 2014, Passed the Assembly A6755 of 2015\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect on the one hundred eightieth day after it\nshall have become a law and shall apply to applications for licensure as\na professional engineer received on or after such effective date;\nprovided however that the commissioner may immediately undertake all\nactions necessary to implement this act upon said effective date includ-\ning but not limited to the adoption of implementing regulations.\n",
